      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine two things: the probability that exactly two of the dice show an even number, and the probability that each die has an even number.\nFor the first part, the probability that a single die shows an even number is 6/12, since there are 6 even numbers out of 12 faces.\nFor the second part, the probability that each die has an even number is (6/12)(6/12)(6/12)(6/12)(6/12), since we want all 5 dice to show an even number.\nTo find the probability that exactly two of the dice show an even number, we can use the binomial probability formula. The binomial probability formula states that the probability of exactly k successes in n trials is given by $\\binom{k}{n}p^k(1-p)^{n-k}$, where $\\binom{k}{n}$ is the binomial coefficient, p is the probability of success, and n is the number of trials.\nIn this case, k=2, n=5, so we can plug these values into the formula.\n$\\binom{2}{5}=\\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!}=10$\n$p=\\frac{1}{2}$ since each die has an even probability of showing an even number, so $p^2=(\\frac{1}{2})^2=\\frac{1}{4}$\n$(1-p)^{5-2}=(1-\\frac{1}{2})^3=\\frac{1}{8}$\nPutting these values into the binomial probability formula, we get $10\\times\\frac{1}{4}\\times\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{10}{32}=\\boxed{\\frac{5}{16}}$.",
